Notebook[{Cell["TMD beam and soft functions, step 1: the Wilson-line model, \
diagrams and amplitudes", "Title"], Cell["This process computes the NLO \
matching coefficients of the quark and gluon TMD beam functions (I_qq, I_qg, \
I_gq, I_gg), the NLO vacuum soft function (quark and gluon), and from them \
the TMD parton distributions f_ij = I_ij Sqrt[S_i].  All of them are matrix \
elements of light-cone operators with Wilson lines, not scattering \
amplitudes.  This step shows how such operator matrix elements are generated \
with FeynArts, checks that the generated currents are gauge invariant, draws \
the diagrams, and converts the amplitudes into the plain records that the \
three algebra routes read in step 2.\n\nThe operators.  With two light-like \
vectors n and nb (n^2 = nb^2 = 0, n.nb = 2) and an incoming parton of \
momentum p along nb (so that n.p is its large light-cone component),\n\n   \
quark:  O_q(b) = qbar(b) W_n(b)^dagger n-slash W_n(0) q(0),      W_n(x) = P \
exp(-i gs Integral_0^Infinity ds n.A(x + s n)),\n   gluon:  O_g(b) = n_nu \
F^{nu mu, a}(b) W_n^A(b)^{ab dagger} W_n^A(0)^{bc} n_rho F^{rho}_mu^{c}(0),\n \
  soft:   O_S(b) = Tr[ (W_n^dagger W_nb)(b)^dagger (W_n^dagger W_nb)(0) ] / \
Nc,\n\nand the beam function is B(x, b_T) = Integral over b^- of e^{-i x n.p \
b^-} <p| O(b) |p>, normalized so that B = delta(1-x) at lowest order; the \
soft function is the vacuum expectation value of O_S, equal to 1 at lowest \
order (see docs/CONVENTIONS.md).  Inserting a complete set of states, <p| \
O(b) |p> = Sum over X of <p| O^dagger-part |X> <X| O-part(0) |p>, so what \
must be generated is the amplitude <X| W_n(0) q(0) |p>, i.e. the process p -> \
X + \"operator\".  The Wilson line runs from the operator point to \
infinity.\n\nThe model.  models/WilsonLineQCD.mod (classes) and \
models/WilsonLine.gen (generic) implement QCD plus:\n  - Wilson-line fields \
S[1] = W_n, S[2] = W_nb (fundamental), S[3] = W_n^A, S[4] = W_nb^A (adjoint), \
drawn as dashed lines with an arrow.  Their Feynman rules are those of a \
heavy quark with velocity n (HQET): propagator i/(n.q), vertex -i gs T^a \
n^mu.  The far end of a Wilson line at infinity is an external leg carrying \
no momentum.\n  - \"sink\" fields that represent the operator vertex: O_q = \
F[2] (carries the open Dirac index of the quark operator), O_g = V[2] (the \
open Lorentz index of F^{nu mu}), O_S = S[5].  They are external legs with a \
single coupling each: O_q q W_n, O_g g W_n^A (plus the two-gluon term of the \
field strength), O_S W_n W_nb.  The couplings gOp and gSoft only mark the \
operator insertion and are divided out.\nIn the diagrams the parton enters \
from the left, the operator leg points up, the Wilson line leaves to the \
right.  The precise Feynman rules and sign conventions are explained in the \
two model files.\n\nMomenta: p incoming parton; k the emitted parton (gluon, \
or quark for the g -> q channel); q = p - k the momentum flowing into the \
operator; w = 0 the momentum of the Wilson-line end at infinity.  For the \
soft function the operator O_S carries the momentum k of the emitted gluon \
and both Wilson-line ends carry zero momentum.", "Text"], 
  Cell["Load the package", "Section"], Cell["Get[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirect\
ory[], \"..\", \"..\", \"FeynFacet\", \"FeynFacet.wl\"}]]", "Input"], 
  Cell["LoadFeynCalc[]", "Input"], Cell["$ModelPath = Prepend[$ModelPath, \
FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"models\"}]];\n$ShapeDataDir = \
FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"models\", \"ShapeData\"}];   (* layouts \
of the diagrams, see models/ShapeData *)\nFileNames[\"*.mod\" | \"*.gen\", \
FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"models\"}]]", "Input"], 
  Cell["Generate the diagrams", "Section"], Cell["Every operator matrix \
element is generated as the process \"parton -> operator leg + Wilson-line \
end + emitted parton\".  In FeynArts notation F[1] is the quark, V[1] the \
gluon, F[2], V[2], S[5] the operator legs and S[1], S[3] the Wilson-line \
ends; a minus sign denotes the antiparticle (the outgoing antiquark in g -> q \
qbar, the Wilson line W_nb of the soft operator whose colour charge flows in \
from infinity).  The one-loop (virtual) corrections are generated too, only \
to be drawn: with p^2 = n^2 = 0 the loop integrals have no scale and vanish \
in dimensional regularization, so the NLO result comes from the real emission \
alone.", "Text"], Cell["generate[loops_, process_] := \
InsertFields[CreateTopologies[loops, Length[process[[1]]] -> \
Length[process[[2]]],\n    ExcludeTopologies -> If[loops == 0, {}, {Tadpoles, \
WFCorrections}]], process,\n  InsertionLevel -> {Classes}, Model -> \
\"WilsonLineQCD\", GenericModel -> \"WilsonLine\"];\n\ndiagrams = <|\n  \
\"BornQ\"  -> generate[0, {F[1]} -> {F[2], S[1]}],                (* q -> O_q \
W_n *)\n  \"BeamQQ\" -> generate[0, {F[1]} -> {F[2], S[1], V[1]}],          \
(* q -> O_q W_n g *)\n  \"BeamQG\" -> generate[0, {V[1]} -> {F[2], S[1], \
-F[1]}],         (* g -> O_q W_n qbar *)\n  \"BornG\"  -> generate[0, {V[1]} \
-> {V[2], S[3]}],                (* g -> O_g W_n^A *)\n  \"BeamGQ\" -> \
generate[0, {F[1]} -> {V[2], S[3], F[1]}],          (* q -> O_g W_n^A q *)\n  \
\"BeamGG\" -> generate[0, {V[1]} -> {V[2], S[3], V[1]}],          (* g -> O_g \
W_n^A g *)\n  \"SoftQ\"  -> generate[0, {S[5]} -> {S[1], -S[2], V[1]}],       \
  (* O_S -> W_n W_nb g, fundamental *)\n  \"SoftG\"  -> generate[0, {S[5]} -> \
{S[3], -S[4], V[1]}]|>;       (* the same with adjoint Wilson lines \
*)\nvirtualDiagrams = DiagramSelect[generate[1, {F[1]} -> {F[2], S[1]}], \
FreeQ[LoopFields[##], F[2] | V[2] | S[5]] &];\nLength /@ Append[diagrams, \
\"VirtualQ\" -> virtualDiagrams]", "Input"], Cell["Expected counts.  Quark \
operator: one Born graph; two real graphs (gluon from the quark, gluon from \
the Wilson line); two g -> q graphs (the gluon splits into q qbar, or the \
Wilson line absorbs the gluon and the operator creates the antiquark).  Gluon \
operator: one Born graph; one q -> g graph; four g -> g graphs (three-gluon \
vertex, two-gluon term of the field strength, emission from and absorption by \
the Wilson line).  Soft: two graphs each (emission from either line).  \
Virtual: the one vertex graph (self-energy insertions are excluded; the \
selection removes graphs in which an operator leg would propagate inside the \
loop).", "Text"], Cell["Require[Length /@ Values[diagrams] === {1, 2, 2, 1, \
1, 4, 2, 2} && Length[virtualDiagrams] === 1, \"Unexpected number of \
diagrams\"];", "Input"], Cell["Draw the diagrams", "Section"], 
  Cell["The generated figures are written to Results/figures/ as PDF files.  \
The figures/ directory contains the shipped previews.  The layout of each \
topology is prescribed in models/ShapeData (FeynArts' own shape files, \
editable with Shape[]).", "Text"], Cell["figures = \
FileNameJoin[{ResultsDirectory[], \"figures\"}];\nIf[!DirectoryQ[figures], \
CreateDirectory[figures, CreateIntermediateDirectories -> \
True]];\ndraw[name_, tops_] := Module[{n = Length[tops], columns, picture},\n \
 columns = Min[n, 4];\n  picture = Paint[tops, ColumnsXRows -> {columns, \
Ceiling[n/columns]}, Numbering -> Simple, SheetHeader -> None,\n    AutoEdit \
-> False, ImageSize -> {170 columns, 170 Ceiling[n/columns]}, DisplayFunction \
-> Identity];\n  Export[FileNameJoin[{figures, name <> \".pdf\"}], \
picture];\n  picture];\ndraw[\"VirtualQ\", \
virtualDiagrams];\nColumn[KeyValueMap[Column[{Style[#1, Bold], draw[#1, #2]}] \
&, diagrams]]", "Input"], Cell["Amplitudes", "Section"], 
  Cell["CreateFeynAmp gives the amplitudes; FCFAConvert rewrites them in \
FeynCalc notation without contracting anything.  The operator leg keeps the \
symbolic momentum q (its spinor or polarization vector is the placeholder for \
the open index); everywhere else q is replaced by p - k and the Wilson-line \
end momentum w by 0.", "Text"], Cell["convert[tops_, incoming_, outgoing_] := \
Module[{amp},\n  amp = FCFAConvert[CreateFeynAmp[tops, Truncated -> False, \
PreFactor -> 1],\n    IncomingMomenta -> incoming, OutgoingMomenta -> \
outgoing, UndoChiralSplittings -> True,\n    ChangeDimension -> D, List -> \
True, SMP -> False, Contract -> False, DropSumOver -> True];\n  amp /. \
{Spinor[Momentum[q, d___], r__] :> Spinor[Momentum[qOperator, d], r], \
Polarization[q, r__] :> Polarization[qOperator, r]} /.\n    {q -> p - k, w -> \
0} /. qOperator -> q];\namplitudes = <|\n  \"BornQ\"  -> \
convert[diagrams[\"BornQ\"], {p}, {q, w}] /. k -> 0,       (* the operator \
carries q = p *)\n  \"BeamQQ\" -> convert[diagrams[\"BeamQQ\"], {p}, {q, w, \
k}],\n  \"BeamQG\" -> convert[diagrams[\"BeamQG\"], {p}, {q, w, k}],\n  \
\"BornG\"  -> convert[diagrams[\"BornG\"], {p}, {q, w}] /. k -> 0,\n  \
\"BeamGQ\" -> convert[diagrams[\"BeamGQ\"], {p}, {q, w, k}],\n  \"BeamGG\" -> \
convert[diagrams[\"BeamGG\"], {p}, {q, w, k}],\n  \"SoftQ\"  -> \
convert[diagrams[\"SoftQ\"], {k}, {w, w, k}],\n  \"SoftG\"  -> \
convert[diagrams[\"SoftG\"], {k}, {w, w, k}]|>;\namplitudes[\"BeamQQ\"]", 
   "Input"], Cell["Each amplitude is linear in the operator marker gOp or \
gSoft; FAEikonal[q, n] stands for the eikonal denominator 1/(n.q).", "Text"], 
  Cell["Require[And @@ Flatten[Map[Exponent[# /. Dot -> Times, gOp] + \
Exponent[# /. Dot -> Times, gSoft] === 1 &, Values[amplitudes], {2}]],\n  \
\"An amplitude is not linear in the operator marker\"];", "Input"], 
  Cell["Check: gauge invariance of the generated currents", "Section"], 
  Cell["The operators are gauge invariant, so the sum of the graphs of each \
process must vanish when the polarization vector of a gluon is replaced by \
its momentum, the other external particles being physical: k_mu J^mu = 0.  \
This is a non-trivial check of the eikonal Feynman rules, of the orientation \
of the Wilson lines and of the relative signs of the field-strength \
couplings: the quark-emission and Wilson-line-emission graphs cancel only if \
the eikonal vertex, the eikonal propagator and the operator vertex are \
consistent.  The operator index is left open, so the identity holds for every \
component of the operator.\n\nKinematics: p^2 = k^2 = n^2 = nb^2 = 0, n.nb = \
2; e is the polarization vector of the other gluon, transverse to its \
momentum.", "Text"], Cell["FCClearScalarProducts[];\nSPD[p] = 0; SPD[k] = 0; \
SPD[n] = 0; SPD[nb] = 0; SPD[n, nb] = 2;\nSPD[p, n] = pn; SPD[k, n] = kn; \
SPD[p, k] = pk; SPD[k, nb] = kb; SPD[p, nb] = pb;\nSPD[e, n] = en; SPD[e, nb] \
= eb; SPD[e, p] = ep; SPD[e, k] = ek; SPD[e] = ee2;\neikonal = \
FAEikonal[mom_, v_] :> 1/ExpandScalarProduct[SPD[v, mom]];\ncurrent[name_, \
replacements_] := \
Factor[DiracSimplify[Contract[ExpandScalarProduct[FeynAmpDenominatorExplicit[\
\n  Total[amplitudes[name]] /. eikonal /. replacements]]]]];\nwardIdentities \
= <|\n  \"BeamQQ: gluon k\" -> current[\"BeamQQ\", Momentum[Polarization[k, \
-I], D] -> Momentum[k, D]],\n  \"BeamQG: gluon p\" -> current[\"BeamQG\", \
Momentum[Polarization[p, I], D] -> Momentum[p, D]],\n  \"BeamGG: gluon k, \
gluon p physical\" -> current[\"BeamGG\", {Momentum[Polarization[q, -I], D] \
-> LorentzIndex[mu, D],\n    Momentum[Polarization[k, -I], D] -> Momentum[k, \
D], Momentum[Polarization[p, I], D] -> Momentum[e, D]}] /. ep -> 0,\n  \
\"BeamGG: gluon p, gluon k physical\" -> current[\"BeamGG\", \
{Momentum[Polarization[q, -I], D] -> LorentzIndex[mu, D],\n    \
Momentum[Polarization[p, I], D] -> Momentum[p, D], Momentum[Polarization[k, \
-I], D] -> Momentum[e, D]}] /. ek -> 0,\n  \"SoftQ: gluon k\" -> \
current[\"SoftQ\", Momentum[Polarization[k, -I], D] -> Momentum[k, D]],\n  \
\"SoftG: gluon k\" -> current[\"SoftG\", Momentum[Polarization[k, -I], D] -> \
Momentum[k, D]]|>;\nRequire[And @@ (# === 0 & /@ Values[wardIdentities]), \"A \
Ward identity fails\", wardIdentities];\nwardIdentities", "Input"], 
  Cell["(The q -> g channel has no external gluon; its single graph needs no \
check.)  A deliberately wrong model, e.g. with the opposite sign of the \
eikonal vertex or of the two-gluon operator coupling, fails these identities; \
they were used to fix the conventions of the model files.", "Text"], 
  Cell["Records", "Section"], Cell["AmplitudeRecords turns each amplitude \
into a list of records, <|\"Coefficient\" -> ..., \"Chains\" -> \
{Chain[...]}|> (vocabulary in FeynFacet/Amplitudes.wl).  The open Dirac index \
of the quark operator appears as the spinor Ubar[q] on the left of the chain; \
the open Lorentz index of the gluon operator as the polarization vector \
PolVector[q, mu, -1]; the eikonal denominators as Eikonal[k, n] = 1/(n.k).", 
   "Text"], Cell["FCClearScalarProducts[];           (* the kinematics of the \
Ward check must not enter the records *)\nrecords = Map[AmplitudeRecords, \
amplitudes, {2}];\nShowRecords[Flatten[records[\"BeamQQ\"]]]", "Input"], 
  Cell["ShowRecords[Flatten[records[\"BeamGG\"]]]", "Input"], 
  Cell["ShowRecords[Flatten[records[\"SoftQ\"]]]", "Input"], 
  Cell["SaveAmplitudes[<|\"FeynCalc\" -> amplitudes, \"Records\" -> \
records|>];\nFileNameJoin[{ResultsDirectory[], \"Amplitudes.wl\"}]", 
   "Input"]}, WindowSize -> {1000, 800}]
